<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Daniel</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Belteshazzar, Dāniyāl</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Daniel is the main character of the Book of Daniel, which tells of a noble Jewish youth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> taken into captivity by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, who served the king and his successors until the time of the Persian conqueror Cyrus while remaining true to the God of Israel. In its tales he interprets dreams and the message written on a wall at the feast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belshazzar">Belshazzar</a>; in its visions he sees the future of the world and of Israel. Most scholars hold that he was not a historical figure, that the tales date from the 3rd or early 2nd century BCE and the visions from between 167 and 164 BCE, alluding to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iv-epiphanes">Antiochus IV Epiphanes</a>. Six cities claim his tomb, the best known at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/daniel-biblical-figure.jpg" alt="Daniel" width="330" height="223" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Peter Paul Rubens · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Peter_Paul_Rubens_-_Daniel_in_the_Lions'_Den_-_Google_Art_Project.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
